"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[7044],{7044:function(t){t.exports=JSON.parse('{"content":"\\n<center>\\n<BookCard \\n          cover=\\"/assets/snippets/books/the-salvation-of-the-saint.png\\"\\n          title=\\"Salvation of a Saint\\"\\n          author=\\"Keigo Higashino\\"\\n          publisher=\\"Jaein\\"\\n          publishedDate=\\"2009-12-28\\"\\n          isbn=\\"9788990982360\\"\\n          rating=4.5\\n          tags=\\"mystery\\"\\n        />\\n</center>\\n\\n## Review\\nAs the second full-length novel of ```Detective Galileo```, it is a work that focuses more on the most classic proposition of mystery: <mark>how did they kill</mark>.  \\n<br>\\nThe two core keys that penetrate this work are the concepts of <mark>Imaginary Solution</mark> and <mark>Salvation</mark>.  \\n<mark>Imaginary Solution</mark> symbolizes a ```solution that is theoretically possible but can never exist in reality```, namely a perfect crime.  \\nOn the other hand, <mark>Salvation</mark> holds the contradictory meanings of both a merciful act of sparing a life and a cold preparation process for certain judgment.  \\n<br>\\nIt seems to be a work that shakes the conventions of this genre in that it\'s about the ```effort not to kill``` rather than the effort to kill.  \\n<br>\\nThere are characters who approach a single goal, the truth, in different ways.  \\n<br>\\nPersonally, I think it\'s one of the most bold and ingenious tricks."}')}}]);